export const PSP1={title:'The event and the rubric',hook:'Five minutes, one topic and no slides—just you, your words and the judges. Prepared Speaking challenges every competitor to speak on the same national theme, so success depends on a fresh perspective, a clear structure and confident delivery. The 2026–27 topic is “Redefine the Standard.”',objectives:['Describe the Prepared Speaking event format and rules.','Explain the 105-point rubric.','Identify the official resource and topic.','Plan a preparation timeline.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Prepared Speaking is a **solo event** open to **middle school**, **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** members. Competitors deliver a **prepared speech of up to five minutes** on the **national topic**, which aligns with the **HOSA membership theme** announced at the end of the previous ILC. For **2026–27**, the topic is **“Redefine the Standard.”**',
'A timekeeper shows a card at **one minute remaining**, and you are stopped at five minutes. **Notecards** (paper or electronic) are allowed, but electronic notes may not be shown to judges, and **no props or other materials** are allowed. There are **no microphones**, so you must project your voice.',
'Bring photo ID and wear business attire or the official HOSA uniform for bonus points.',
'Because everyone speaks on the same topic, originality and depth of thought are what set speeches apart.',
'The event is sponsored by eDynamic Learning, which offers a discounted online public speaking course for HOSA members.',
flip('Event facts',[['Topic','Redefine the Standard'],['Length','Up to 5 minutes'],['Time card','One minute remaining'],['Notecards','Allowed; electronic not shown'],['Props','Not allowed'],['Microphones','None']])]],
['The rubric',[
'The speech is scored out of **105 points**. **Content (60):** coverage of the topic, impact (a strong and meaningful message), appropriateness to the conference theme and a clear focus and point of view—**15 points each**. **Organization (30):** opening statement, cohesion of the body and closing—**10 points each**. **Delivery (15):** voice, stage presence and diction—**5 points each**.',
'Notice that content carries more than half the points. A polished delivery cannot rescue a vague message, but a strong message delivered clearly can score very well.',
'Ties are broken by the rubric sections with the highest point values, so content matters most there too.',
'Print the rating sheet and use it to evaluate every draft and rehearsal.',
'Each judge scores independently, so aim to make every item obvious and strong.',
{t:'table',caption:'Prepared Speaking rubric',head:['Section','Items','Points'],rows:[['Content','Coverage, impact, theme, focus (15 each)','60'],['Organization','Opening, cohesion, closing (10 each)','30'],['Delivery','Voice, stage presence, diction (5 each)','15'],['Total','','105']]}]],
['What judges want in content',[
'**Coverage of topic** rewards command of the subject—accurate, well-developed ideas. **Impact** rewards a strong, meaningful message that stays with listeners. **Appropriate to the conference theme** checks that your speech clearly connects to “Redefine the Standard.” **Clear focus and point of view** rewards a speech built around one central idea rather than a collection of loosely related thoughts.',
'Together, these items ask: Do you know your topic? Does your message matter? Is it about the theme? Is it focused?',
'A speech that wanders between many unrelated ideas loses focus points even if each idea is interesting.',
'Decide early what single message you want judges to remember.',
'Every story, statistic and example should support that message.',
"Listen to past winning speeches if available through your advisor to understand the level of depth and polish judges expect.",
match('Content item and what it rewards',[['Coverage of topic','Command of the subject'],['Impact','A strong, meaningful message'],['Appropriate to theme','Clear link to Redefine the Standard'],['Clear focus','One central idea and point of view'],['Supporting material','Evidence and examples that build the message'],['Originality','A fresh perspective on a shared topic']])]],
['Organization and delivery',[
'**Organization** rewards an **opening** that clearly establishes the topic and grabs attention, a **body** that is logical and cohesive with smooth transitions and a **closing** that prepares the audience for the end and leaves a lasting impression.',
'**Delivery** rewards a voice that is loud enough to hear with varied rate and volume, purposeful movement and gestures with comfortable body language and clear diction and pronunciation without vocal fillers.',
'Without a microphone, projection and pacing are especially important.',
'Practice in a room similar in size to a competition room so you learn how loudly to speak.',
'Strong organization also makes delivery easier, because you always know what comes next.',
sort('Organization or delivery?',['Organization','Delivery'],[['A gripping opening line',0],['Varying vocal volume',1],['Smooth transitions between points',0],['Purposeful gestures',1],['A memorable closing',0],['Clear pronunciation',1]])]],
['Official resource and research',[
'The official resource is the **eDynamic Learning online public speaking course**, available to HOSA members at a discount. It applies to many events with speaking components.',
'For content, use credible sources—health organizations, government agencies, peer-reviewed research and reputable news—and real experiences from your life and community.',
'Keep a source list so you can mention sources aloud when you cite facts.',
'Read HOSA’s own description of the membership theme to understand its intended spirit.',
'Combine official theme materials with your own perspective to create an original speech.',
chk('What is the official resource listed for Prepared Speaking?',['The eDynamic Learning public speaking course','A specific textbook on anatomy','The HOSA Bowl test','No resource is listed'],'Sponsor course.')]],
['A preparation timeline',[
'**Early fall:** study the theme, brainstorm angles and choose a central message. **Fall:** research and gather stories, data and examples. **Winter:** outline, draft and revise the speech; test it with listeners. **Spring:** rehearse regularly, refine delivery and time it precisely.',
'A speech improves with each revision, so start early enough to rewrite several times.',
'Schedule regular rehearsals in front of people, not just alone.',
'Record rehearsals to monitor timing and filler words.',
'Leave the final weeks for polishing delivery rather than writing new content.',
order('Prepared Speaking timeline',['Study the theme','Brainstorm angles','Choose a central message','Research supporting material','Outline and draft','Revise with feedback','Rehearse and time the speech'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Event rules',{a:{text:'You want to hold up a photo during your speech. (Scenario invented for practice.) Is that allowed?',choices:[['No—props and materials other than notecards are not allowed','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, photos are fine','b','Only notecards are permitted.',false]]},b:{text:'Your rehearsal runs 5 minutes 40 seconds. What happens at competition?',choices:[['You will be stopped at 5 minutes—cut the speech to about 4:45','c','Correct.',true],['Judges will let you finish','c','Time is strictly enforced.',false]]},c:{text:'You trim your speech and rely on words, not props.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Prepared Speaking is a solo event (middle school, secondary, postsecondary) with a prepared speech of up to 5 minutes on the 2026–27 topic “Redefine the Standard”; card at one minute; notecards only; no props or microphones.','The 105-point rubric: content 60 (coverage, impact, theme, focus at 15 each), organization 30 (opening, cohesion, closing at 10 each) and delivery 15 (voice, stage presence, diction at 5 each).','Use the eDynamic Learning course and credible sources, start early and revise and rehearse with a timer and listeners.'],terms:[['National topic','The theme every competitor speaks on, aligned with the HOSA membership theme.'],['Cohesion','How logically and smoothly the parts of a speech connect.'],['Projection','Speaking loudly and clearly enough to be heard without a microphone.']],mcqs:[q('What is the 2026–27 Prepared Speaking topic?','Redefine the Standard','Healthy Living for All','Lead the Way','The Future Is Now','Topic.'),q('What is the maximum speech length?','5 minutes','3 minutes','10 minutes','7 minutes','Rule.'),q('How many points is content worth?','60','30','15','105','Rubric.'),q('Which item is worth 15 points?','Impact','Opening statement','Voice','Closing','Rubric.'),q('Are microphones used?','No','Yes','Only for finals','Only on request','Rule.'),q('Which materials are allowed?','Notecards','Slides','Props','Posters','Rule.'),q('Which divisions may compete?','Middle school, secondary and postsecondary/collegiate','Secondary only','Postsecondary only','Middle school only','Eligibility.'),q('When is the time card shown?','With one minute remaining','At the start','At three minutes','Never','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Prepared Speaking event and how its rubric should shape your preparation.',rubric:[['Event',['redefine the standard','5 minutes','one minute','notecards','no props','no microphones']],['Content',['60','coverage','impact','theme','focus','point of view']],['Organization and delivery',['opening','cohesion','closing','voice','stage presence','diction']],['Preparation',['edynamic','research','revise','rehearse','timeline','feedback']]],model:'Prepared Speaking is a solo event for middle school, secondary and postsecondary members in which every competitor gives a prepared speech of up to five minutes on the same national topic, which for 2026–27 is “Redefine the Standard,” aligned with the HOSA membership theme. A timekeeper shows a card at one minute remaining and stops the speech at five minutes; notecards are allowed but no props, and there are no microphones, so projection matters. The 105-point rubric gives 60 points to content—coverage of the topic, impact, appropriateness to the conference theme and a clear focus and point of view at 15 points each—30 points to organization, with the opening, cohesion of the body and closing at 10 each, and 15 points to delivery, with voice, stage presence and diction at 5 each. Because content carries more than half the points and everyone speaks on the same topic, I would focus first on developing one original, meaningful message clearly connected to the theme, supported by credible research and real stories. Then I would organize the speech with a strong opening, a cohesive body with smooth transitions and a memorable closing. I would use the eDynamic Learning public speaking course, start brainstorming in early fall, draft and revise in winter with feedback from listeners and rehearse throughout spring with a timer and recordings, aiming for about 4 minutes 45 seconds and practicing projection in a competition-sized room.'},sources:[RULES,EDL]};

export const PSP2={title:'Interpreting “Redefine the Standard”',hook:'A standard is a level of quality or an expectation we measure ourselves against—a standard of care, a standard of excellence, a personal standard. To “redefine” it is to question, raise or reimagine it. Because every competitor receives the same theme, your interpretation is your first chance to stand out.',objectives:['Analyze the key words of the theme.','Brainstorm multiple interpretations connected to health care.','Choose an angle that is original and meaningful.','Connect your interpretation to HOSA’s mission.'],sections:[
['Unpacking the words',[
'Start with the words themselves. A **standard** can be a **benchmark of quality** (standards of care), a **rule or requirement** (professional standards, safety standards), a **norm or expectation** (what is considered “normal”) or a **personal principle** (the standards you hold yourself to). To **redefine** means to **change the meaning**, **raise the bar**, **challenge assumptions** or **create a new model**.',
'Writing several definitions helps you see the many directions the theme can take.',
'Look up both words in a dictionary and note different senses you could explore.',
'Ask what standards exist in health care, in HOSA and in your own life.',
'Think about who sets standards, who benefits and who might be left out.',
match('Meaning of “standard” and example',[['Benchmark of quality','Standards of patient care'],['Rule or requirement','Infection control standards'],['Norm or expectation','What patients expect from a clinic visit'],['Personal principle','Your own standard of integrity'],['Measure of success','How health outcomes are judged'],['Professional code','Ethical standards for health workers']])]],
['Brainstorming angles',[
'Brainstorm widely before choosing. Possible angles include: **raising standards of care** through compassion and safety; **redefining what “healthy” means** to include mental health; **redefining access** so quality care reaches everyone; **redefining leadership** in health care to include young people; **redefining professionalism** with empathy and cultural humility; or **redefining your personal standard** of effort and service.',
'Aim for at least ten ideas, then group similar ones.',
'Do not judge ideas too early—unusual angles often become the most memorable speeches.',
'Ask friends, family and health professionals what the phrase means to them.',
'Note real examples that fit each angle, since examples will support your speech.',
sort('Health care angle or off-topic?',['Health care angle','Off-topic'],[['Redefining access to quality care in rural communities',0],['My favorite video game’s new rules',1],['Raising the standard of compassion in patient care',0],['Fashion trends this year',1],['Redefining “healthy” to include mental health',0],['Redefining student leadership in HOSA',0]])]],
['Choosing an original angle',[
'Many competitors will choose the most obvious interpretation, such as “raise the bar” in general terms. To stand out, choose an angle that is **specific**, **personal** or **unexpected**—for example, redefining the standard of listening in health care, inspired by a family member’s experience.',
'An original angle still must connect clearly to the theme; judges score appropriateness to the conference theme at 15 points.',
'Test your angle by asking: Can I explain it in one sentence? Do I care about it? Can I support it with evidence and stories?',
'Choose an angle you can speak about with genuine passion.',
'Specific angles also make research and organization easier.',
"Avoid choosing an angle only because it sounds impressive. Judges respond most to speakers who clearly care about their message.",
ex('Testing an angle','Test a possible angle (example).',['Angle: redefining the standard of patient listening','One sentence: “Real care begins when we listen as well as we treat.”','Personal connection: a grandparent who felt unheard in the hospital','Evidence: research on communication and patient outcomes','Theme link: listening as a new standard of care'],'A specific, personal, supportable angle clearly tied to the theme.')]],
['Connecting to HOSA',[
'HOSA’s mission is to empower HOSA-Future Health Professionals to become leaders in the global health community through **education, collaboration and experience**. Connecting your interpretation to future health professionals—your audience—makes your message relevant.',
'Ask: How can HOSA members redefine the standard in their schools, communities and future careers? A speech that ends with a call to action for members often has strong impact.',
'Using HOSA’s theme materials helps you understand how the organization frames the idea.',
'Avoid simply repeating the theme phrase; explain what it means in action.',
'Members in the audience should see themselves in your message.',
chk('Why connect your interpretation to HOSA members?',['It makes the message relevant to the audience of future health professionals','It replaces the need for evidence','It shortens the speech','It is required to mention HOSA ten times'],'Audience relevance.')]],
['Avoiding common pitfalls',[
'Common pitfalls include being **too broad** (“We should redefine every standard”), **too vague** (“Standards are important”), **off-theme** (a general speech about health with no link to standards) and **clichéd** (relying on overused quotes or slogans).',
'Fix these by narrowing your focus, using specific examples and explaining exactly what the new standard is and why it matters.',
'Define the old standard and the new one clearly so listeners understand the change you are proposing.',
'Make sure your speech answers: What standard? Why redefine it? How?',
'Specificity is the antidote to most pitfalls.',
"Read your thesis aloud to someone unfamiliar with the theme. If they understand what standard you are redefining and why, your framing is clear.",
sort('Strong or weak framing?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“The old standard measured care by speed; the new standard measures it by trust.”',0],['“Standards are important.”',1],['“We must redefine access so a zip code never decides care.”',0],['“Let’s redefine everything!”',1],['“I will define what listening means in a hospital room.”',0]])]],
['From interpretation to message',[
'Once you choose an angle, write a one-sentence **central message**: the idea you want judges to remember. For example: “The new standard of care is not just treating illness—it is making every patient feel heard.”',
'Your central message guides every part of your speech—opening, body and closing.',
'If a story or fact does not support the message, leave it out.',
'Share your message with a few listeners and ask them to repeat it back. If they can, it is clear.',
'Revise the wording until it is short, memorable and specific.',
order('From theme to message',['Unpack the words','Brainstorm many angles','Choose a specific, original angle','Connect to HOSA members','Write a one-sentence central message','Test it with listeners'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Interpreting the theme',{a:{text:'Your first draft says, “We should always try our best and redefine standards.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the problem?',choices:[['It is vague—define which standard, why and how to redefine it','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing; it is inspirational','b','Specificity earns focus and coverage points.',false]]},b:{text:'You choose “redefining the standard of listening in patient care.” How do you test it?',choices:[['Write it in one sentence and check you can support it with stories and evidence','c','Correct.',true],['Change angles daily','c','Test and commit to a strong angle.',false]]},c:{text:'You have a clear, original message tied to the theme.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['“Standard” can mean a benchmark of quality, rule, norm, personal principle or professional code; “redefine” means changing, raising, challenging or reimagining it.','Brainstorm many health care angles (care, access, defining health, leadership, professionalism, personal standards) and choose one that is specific, personal or unexpected but clearly on theme.','Connect to HOSA members and the mission, avoid broad, vague or clichéd framing, define old and new standards and write a one-sentence central message.'],terms:[['Standard','A level of quality, rule or expectation used as a measure.'],['Angle','The specific perspective you take on a topic.'],['Central message','The one idea you want listeners to remember.']],mcqs:[q('What does “redefine” mean in the theme?','Change, raise or reimagine the meaning','Repeat exactly','Ignore','Delete','Definition.'),q('Which is a health care angle on the theme?','Redefining access to quality care','Fashion trends','Video game rules','Sports scores','On-topic.'),q('Why choose a specific angle?','It stands out and is easier to support','It is required to be vague','It shortens research','Judges prefer generic speeches','Originality.'),q('How many points is appropriateness to the theme worth?','15','5','10','30','Rubric.'),q('Which framing is strongest?','“The old standard measured care by speed; the new standard measures it by trust.”','“Standards are important.”','“Let’s redefine everything!”','“Things should be better.”','Specificity.'),q('A central message should be:','One clear, memorable sentence','A long paragraph','Several unrelated ideas','A quote only','Focus.'),q('HOSA’s mission empowers members through:','Education, collaboration and experience','Competition only','Fundraising','Travel','Mission.'),q('A common pitfall is being:','Too vague','Too specific','Too supported','Too focused','Pitfall.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would interpret “Redefine the Standard” and develop an original central message.',rubric:[['Unpacking',['standard','benchmark','rule','norm','personal','redefine']],['Brainstorming',['angles','care','access','health','leadership','many ideas']],['Choosing',['specific','personal','original','theme','one sentence','evidence']],['Message',['central message','hosa','members','old standard','new standard','test']]],model:'I would begin by unpacking the theme’s words. A standard can be a benchmark of quality such as standards of care, a rule such as infection control requirements, a norm or expectation, a personal principle or a professional code, while redefining means changing its meaning, raising the bar or challenging assumptions. Next, I would brainstorm at least ten health care angles—raising the standard of compassion, redefining what “healthy” means to include mental health, redefining access so care reaches rural communities, redefining leadership to include young people and redefining my own standard of service—and ask family members and a nurse what the phrase means to them. Because many speakers will choose general “raise the bar” messages, I would choose a specific, personal angle: redefining the standard of listening in patient care, inspired by my grandfather, who felt unheard during a hospital stay. I would test it by expressing it in one sentence, confirming I care about it deeply and checking that I can support it with evidence on communication and patient outcomes. I would connect it to HOSA’s mission and to my audience of future health professionals by asking how members can listen better in clinicals, jobs and their communities. To avoid vague or clichéd framing, I would define the old standard—care measured by speed and procedures—and the new one—care measured by whether patients feel heard. My central message would be: “The new standard of care begins when we listen as well as we treat.”'},sources:[RULES,RHET]};

export const PSP3={title:'Point of view, audience and purpose',hook:'Clear focus and point of view is worth 15 points. Judges want to know exactly what you believe and why it matters. That requires understanding your purpose—to inform, persuade or inspire—and your audience: judges and fellow future health professionals who will hear many speeches on the same theme.',objectives:['Define your purpose and point of view.','Analyze the audience and setting.','Use the rhetorical situation to shape your speech.','Maintain one clear focus throughout.'],sections:[
['Purpose of the speech',[
'Most Prepared Speaking speeches aim to **inspire** and **persuade**—to change how listeners think about a standard and motivate them to act. Some also **inform**, sharing facts listeners may not know. Decide your primary purpose; it shapes your tone, structure and closing.',
'An inspirational speech emphasizes stories, values and a call to action. A persuasive speech emphasizes reasons and evidence. Many strong speeches blend both.',
'Write a purpose statement for yourself: “By the end, I want listeners to believe… and to…”',
'Your purpose helps you choose which material to include.',
'Revisit your purpose whenever you revise.',
"Think about the change you want: a new belief, a new feeling or a new action. Naming the change keeps your speech purposeful from start to finish.",
match('Purpose and emphasis',[['Inspire','Stories, values and a call to action'],['Persuade','Reasons and evidence'],['Inform','New facts and explanations'],['Blend','Story plus evidence plus action'],['Purpose statement','What listeners should believe and do'],['Closing','Matches the purpose']])]],
['Point of view',[
'Your **point of view** is your position on the theme—what you believe about the standard and how it should be redefined. A clear point of view is stated early, repeated in key moments and supported throughout.',
'Avoid speeches that list many ideas without taking a stance. Judges score focus and point of view together because a focused speech has one clear perspective.',
'Use first person when sharing personal experiences, but support your view with evidence beyond yourself.',
'State your point of view in your opening and restate it in your closing.',
'A strong point of view makes your speech memorable among many on the same theme.',
"A point of view can acknowledge complexity while still taking a stand. For example, you can admit that time pressure is real and still argue that listening must be protected.",
sort('Clear point of view?',['Clear','Unclear'],[['“I believe the true standard of care is whether patients feel heard.”',0],['“There are many things about standards.”',1],['“Our generation must redefine access as a right, not a privilege.”',0],['“Standards can be good or bad, I guess.”',1],['“Compassion, not speed, should be the measure of quality.”',0]])]],
['Understanding the audience',[
'Your audience includes **judges**—often health professionals, educators or community members—and the broader community of **HOSA members**. They have heard many speeches on the same theme, so originality and authenticity stand out.',
'Consider what they know and value: judges appreciate accurate content and clear organization; HOSA members relate to student experiences and future careers.',
'Use language that is professional yet warm, avoiding jargon unless you explain it.',
'Imagine one listener—a busy nurse judge—and ask what would make them remember your speech.',
'Respect diverse perspectives in your audience.',
"Consider what judges may already believe about the theme. Addressing a common assumption directly can make your perspective feel fresh and thoughtful.",
chk('Why does originality matter especially in Prepared Speaking?',['Judges hear many speeches on the same topic','The topic changes for each speaker','Originality is not scored','Judges dislike evidence'],'Same theme for everyone.')]],
['The rhetorical situation',[
'The **rhetorical situation** includes the **speaker** (you and your credibility), the **audience**, the **purpose**, the **context** (a HOSA competition, a five-minute limit, no microphones or props) and the **message**. Considering all five helps you make smart choices.',
'For example, the five-minute limit means one central message with two or three main points; the lack of visual aids means vivid language and stories must create images.',
'Your credibility grows when you share relevant experience, cite reliable sources and speak confidently.',
'Context also includes the room size, so plan projection accordingly.',
'Each element should support the others.',
"Think about context beyond the room as well. Your message should matter to the health care community your listeners will soon join.",
flip('Rhetorical situation',[['Speaker','You and your credibility'],['Audience','Judges and HOSA members'],['Purpose','Inspire, persuade or inform'],['Context','Five minutes, no props or microphones'],['Message','Your central idea'],['Choices','Shaped by all five elements']])]],
['Building credibility',[
'Listeners trust speakers who show **knowledge**, **honesty** and **goodwill**. Build credibility by sharing relevant experiences (a job shadow, volunteering, a family health experience), citing credible sources aloud (“According to the CDC…”) and acknowledging complexity rather than oversimplifying.',
'Appearance and confident delivery also affect credibility—professional dress and steady eye contact signal preparation.',
"Humility also builds credibility. Saying “I am still learning, but here is what I have seen” can make listeners more open to your message.",
ex('Citing a source aloud','Practice an oral citation (statistic invented for practice).',['Name the source: “According to a 2025 national survey,”','State the fact briefly: “one in three patients said they did not feel heard.”','Connect it: “That is not a statistic—it is a standard waiting to be redefined.”'],'A smooth oral citation that builds credibility (statistic invented for practice).')]],
['Keeping one focus',[
'Every part of the speech should support the central message. After drafting, check each paragraph: Does it advance my point of view? If not, cut or revise it.',
'Use a **focus test**: summarize each section in one sentence and confirm each sentence connects to your message.',
'Two or three main points that build on each other are more powerful than five scattered ideas.',
'Transitions should remind listeners how each point connects to the message.',
'Focus also helps you stay within five minutes.',
order('Focus test',['Write your central message','Summarize each section in one sentence','Check each summary against the message','Cut or revise sections that drift','Strengthen transitions','Re-time the speech'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Focus review',{a:{text:'Your draft includes five main points: listening, technology, nutrition, school stress and sports injuries. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Keep two or three points that support your central message about listening and cut the rest','b','Correct.',true],['Add more points','b','Focus earns points.',false]]},b:{text:'You cite a statistic but do not name the source. How do you improve it?',choices:[['Name the credible source aloud before the statistic','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it—judges will trust you','c','Oral citations build credibility.',false]]},c:{text:'Your speech is focused and credible.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Decide your purpose (inspire, persuade, inform or a blend) and write a purpose statement of what listeners should believe and do.','State a clear point of view early and restate it in the closing; analyze judges and HOSA members as your audience and use the rhetorical situation (speaker, audience, purpose, context, message) to make choices.','Build credibility with relevant experience, oral citations and acknowledged complexity; keep one focus with two or three connected main points and use a focus test to cut drift.'],terms:[['Point of view','Your position or perspective on the topic.'],['Rhetorical situation','Speaker, audience, purpose, context and message.'],['Credibility','The trust listeners place in a speaker.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is clear focus and point of view worth?','15','5','10','30','Rubric.'),q('Which shows a clear point of view?','“I believe the true standard of care is whether patients feel heard.”','“There are many things about standards.”','“Standards can be good or bad.”','“I guess it depends.”','Stance.'),q('Which is part of the rhetorical situation?','Audience','Font size','Paper color','Shoe size','Concept.'),q('How can you build credibility?','Cite credible sources aloud','Avoid evidence','Use slang','Look at the floor','Credibility.'),q('How many main points usually work best in five minutes?','Two or three','Ten','Zero','Twenty','Focus.'),q('What does a focus test check?','That each section supports the central message','Font choices','Room temperature','Judges’ names','Revision.'),q('Without visual aids, speakers should rely on:','Vivid language and stories','Props','Slides','Posters','Context.'),q('A purpose statement describes:','What listeners should believe and do','The speaker’s address','The judges’ scores','The room size','Purpose.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how purpose, audience and point of view shape a focused Prepared Speaking speech.',rubric:[['Purpose',['inspire','persuade','inform','purpose statement','call to action']],['Point of view',['point of view','stance','early','restate','clear']],['Audience and situation',['judges','hosa members','rhetorical situation','context','five minutes','no props']],['Credibility and focus',['credibility','sources','experience','focus test','two or three points']]],model:'My primary purpose would be to inspire and persuade: by the end, I want listeners to believe that the true standard of care is whether patients feel heard and to commit to listening more deeply in their own clinical experiences. That purpose shapes everything: stories and values to inspire, evidence to persuade and a call to action in the closing. My point of view would be clear from the first minute—“I believe the new standard of care begins when we listen as well as we treat”—and I would restate it in the closing. My audience includes judges, often health professionals or educators, and HOSA members, all of whom will hear many speeches on “Redefine the Standard,” so originality and authenticity matter; I would use professional but warm language and explain any medical terms. Considering the rhetorical situation—me as a HOSA member with volunteer experience, an audience of future health professionals, a five-minute limit and no microphones or props—I would limit myself to one central message and three connected main points and use vivid stories to create images without visuals. I would build credibility by sharing my experience volunteering at a hospital, citing sources aloud, such as “According to a national patient survey…,” and acknowledging that listening takes time in busy settings. Finally, I would run a focus test, summarizing each section in one sentence and cutting anything that does not support my message.'},sources:[RULES,RHET]};

export const PSP4={title:'Gathering supporting material',hook:'Coverage of the topic is worth 15 points and rewards a speaker who demonstrates command of the subject. Command comes from strong supporting material—stories that move people, facts that prove points and examples that make ideas concrete. This lesson covers finding, evaluating and using evidence in a speech.',objectives:['Identify types of supporting material for a speech.','Find and evaluate credible sources.','Use personal stories ethically and effectively.','Balance emotion and evidence.'],sections:[
['Types of supporting material',[
'Strong speeches use a mix of support: **personal stories** (your experiences), **examples** (real people, programs or events), **statistics** (numbers that show scope), **expert testimony** (quotes from professionals or organizations), **comparisons and analogies** (making ideas relatable) and **definitions** (clarifying key terms such as “standard of care”).',
'Each type does different work: stories create connection, statistics show scale, experts add authority and analogies simplify complexity.',
'Aim for variety so the speech engages both hearts and minds.',
'Choose the strongest pieces rather than including everything you find.',
'Every piece of support should connect directly to your central message.',
"Interviews with local health professionals provide original quotes and insights no other competitor will have.",
match('Support type and job',[['Personal story','Creates emotional connection'],['Statistic','Shows scope or scale'],['Expert testimony','Adds authority'],['Analogy','Makes complex ideas relatable'],['Example','Makes ideas concrete'],['Definition','Clarifies key terms']])]],
['Finding credible sources',[
'Look for information from **government health agencies** (CDC, NIH, HHS), **international organizations** (WHO), **professional associations**, **peer-reviewed research** and **reputable news outlets**. Interviews with health professionals in your community are also powerful.',
'Evaluate sources by asking who created them, what evidence they provide, when they were published and whether they have a bias or agenda.',
'Prefer recent, specific facts that directly support your point.',
'Record each source so you can cite it accurately aloud.',
'Avoid statistics from unknown websites or social media posts.',
"When possible, find the original source of a statistic rather than citing a secondhand report. Original sources are more reliable.",
sort('Credible source?',['Credible','Questionable'],[['A CDC data brief',0],['An anonymous social media post',1],['A peer-reviewed study',0],['A product advertisement claiming miracle results',1],['An interview with a local nurse',0],['A website with no author or date',1]])]],
['Using statistics effectively',[
'Numbers are powerful when they are **few**, **clear** and **explained**. Round numbers for listeners (“about one in three”), connect them to people (“that is nine students in a class of twenty-seven”) and explain why they matter.',
'Avoid overwhelming listeners with lists of numbers; one strong statistic, well explained, often has more impact than five.',
'Always name the source and, when helpful, the year.',
'Make sure statistics are accurate and not taken out of context.',
'Practice saying numbers clearly—they can be hard to hear without a microphone.',
"Compare statistics to something familiar, such as the number of students in your school, to help listeners grasp scale quickly.",
ex('Making a statistic memorable','Translate a statistic for listeners (statistic invented for practice).',['Original: 33% of patients reported feeling unheard','Round and humanize: “one in three patients”','Make it concrete: “In a waiting room of 30 people, 10 feel invisible.”','Connect: “That is the standard we must redefine.”'],'A clear, memorable statistic that supports the message (statistic invented for practice).')]],
['Personal stories',[
'Personal stories are often the most memorable part of a speech. Choose a story that **directly illustrates your message**, tell it with **specific details**, include a **moment of tension or change** and explain **what it taught you**.',
'Protect privacy: get permission to share someone’s story or change identifying details. Be honest—never invent or exaggerate experiences.',
'A story told well can be the emotional heart of your speech, but it should be followed by evidence that shows the issue goes beyond one person.',
'Keep stories concise—usually under a minute.',
'Practice stories aloud so they sound natural, not memorized.',
"Rehearse your story until you can tell it naturally with eye contact. A story read from notes loses much of its emotional power.",
chk('What should follow a personal story in a persuasive speech?',['Evidence showing the issue extends beyond one person','Another unrelated story','Nothing','A list of jokes'],'Balance emotion and evidence.')]],
['Balancing emotion and logic',[
'Persuasion often combines **ethos** (credibility), **pathos** (emotion) and **logos** (logic and evidence). A speech with only emotion can feel manipulative; a speech with only facts can feel cold. The strongest speeches balance a moving story with clear reasoning and credible evidence.',
'Map your speech to see the balance: where do you create emotion, where do you prove points and where do you build trust?',
'Use emotion to engage listeners and logic to convince them.',
'Credibility connects both by showing you are knowledgeable and sincere.',
'Impact (15 points) often comes from this balance.',
"If your speech feels too emotional, add a fact or expert view; if it feels too dry, add a brief story or image. Balance can be adjusted in revision.",
sort('Ethos, pathos or logos?',['Ethos','Pathos','Logos'],[['Mentioning your volunteer experience',0],['Describing a grandparent’s fear in the hospital',1],['Citing research on communication and outcomes',2],['Quoting a respected physician',0],['Sharing a moment of hope',1],['Explaining cause and effect',2]])]],
['Organizing research notes',[
'Organize notes by **main point**: for each point, list the best story, statistic, example and expert quote. This makes drafting easier and ensures each point is well supported.',
'Keep a separate list of sources with author, title, date and URL for accuracy.',
'Remove material that does not fit your central message, even if it is interesting.',
'Highlight the single strongest piece of support for each point.',
'Revisit your notes as you revise to swap in stronger material.',
order('Research workflow',['List your main points','Search credible sources for each point','Gather stories and interviews','Evaluate and select the strongest support','Organize notes by point','Record sources for oral citation'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Choosing support',{a:{text:'You found a dramatic statistic on an anonymous website. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do?',choices:[['Verify it with a credible source or replace it','b','Correct.',true],['Use it because it is dramatic','b','Credibility matters.',false]]},b:{text:'Your speech is all statistics and feels flat. What would add impact?',choices:[['Add a concise personal story that illustrates your message','c','Correct.',true],['Add more numbers','c','Balance emotion and evidence.',false]]},c:{text:'Your speech balances story and evidence.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Use varied support—personal stories, examples, statistics, expert testimony, analogies and definitions—each connected to the central message.','Evaluate sources (government agencies, WHO, associations, peer-reviewed research, reputable news, local professionals) for author, evidence, date and bias; use few, clear, humanized statistics with sources named aloud.','Tell honest, concise, privacy-respecting stories with tension and lessons; balance ethos, pathos and logos; organize notes by main point and record sources.'],terms:[['Supporting material','Stories, facts and examples that develop a speech’s ideas.'],['Pathos','Persuasion through emotion.'],['Logos','Persuasion through logic and evidence.']],mcqs:[q('Which support type adds authority?','Expert testimony','A joke','A random fact','An unrelated story','Support.'),q('Which source is most credible?','A CDC data brief','An anonymous post','A product ad','A website with no author','Credibility.'),q('How should statistics be used?','Few, clear and explained','As many as possible','Without sources','Unrounded and complex','Statistics.'),q('What should follow a personal story in a persuasive speech?','Evidence that the issue extends beyond one person','Nothing','An unrelated joke','A long list of numbers','Balance.'),q('Pathos refers to:','Emotion','Logic','Credibility','Timing','Rhetoric.'),q('Logos refers to:','Logic and evidence','Emotion','Credibility','Costume','Rhetoric.'),q('How should research notes be organized?','By main point','Randomly','By color only','By length','Organization.'),q('Sharing someone else’s story requires:','Permission or changed identifying details','Nothing','Exaggeration','Their phone number','Ethics.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would gather and use supporting material to demonstrate command of the topic.',rubric:[['Support types',['stories','statistics','examples','expert','analogy','definition']],['Sources',['credible','cdc','peer-reviewed','evaluate','date','bias']],['Using support',['humanize','few','cite aloud','concise story','privacy','honest']],['Balance and organization',['ethos','pathos','logos','balance','organize','main point']]],model:'To demonstrate command of the topic, I would gather varied supporting material for each of my three main points about redefining the standard of listening in patient care. For emotional connection, I would use a concise personal story about my grandfather feeling unheard during a hospital stay, sharing it with my family’s permission and telling it honestly with specific details, a moment of tension and what it taught me. To show scope, I would find a statistic from a credible source, such as a government agency or a peer-reviewed study on patient communication, round it for listeners—“about one in three patients”—and humanize it: “In a waiting room of 30, that is 10 people who feel invisible.” For authority, I would quote a nurse I interviewed at a local hospital and a professional organization’s guidance on patient-centered care, and I would use an analogy comparing listening to a diagnostic tool. I would evaluate each source by asking who created it, what evidence it provides, when it was published and whether it has a bias, rejecting anonymous or advertising sources. I would balance ethos, pathos and logos—my volunteer experience for credibility, my grandfather’s story for emotion and research for logic—so the speech feels both moving and convincing. Finally, I would organize my notes by main point, highlight the strongest support for each and keep a source list so I can cite each fact aloud accurately.'},sources:[RULES,EVAL]};

export const PSP5={title:'Writing a powerful opening',hook:'Judges begin scoring the moment you speak. The opening statement is worth 10 points and should clearly establish the topic and draw listeners in. In the first 30 to 45 seconds, you must capture attention, introduce your point of view and preview where the speech is going.',objectives:['Use proven techniques to capture attention.','Establish the topic and point of view clearly.','Preview the structure of the speech.','Deliver the opening with confidence.'],sections:[
['Why openings matter',[
'An opening sets the tone, earns attention and builds credibility. Judges who are engaged in the first moments listen more closely to everything that follows. The rubric’s top score describes an opening that **clearly establishes the topic** and **grabs attention**.',
'A strong opening also calms your nerves: knowing exactly how you will begin helps you start confidently.',
'Plan the opening carefully and rehearse it more than any other part.',
'Avoid starting with filler such as “Hi, my speech is about…”',
'Your first sentence should make listeners want the second.',
chk('Which opening is weakest?',['“Hi, my speech is about standards.”','A vivid moment from a hospital room','A surprising statistic with a source','A thought-provoking question'],'Avoid generic openings.')]],
['Attention-grabbing techniques',[
'Effective techniques include a **vivid story** (“The monitor beeped, but no one looked up”), a **surprising fact** (“One in three patients leaves a visit feeling unheard”), a **thought-provoking question** (“What if the measure of good care were not how fast we treat, but how well we listen?”), a **meaningful quote** used sparingly or a **bold statement** of your point of view.',
'Choose the technique that best fits your message and your strengths as a speaker.',
'Stories work especially well without visual aids because they create mental images.',
'Questions work best when they make listeners think rather than answer aloud.',
'Avoid shocking or graphic openings that distract from your message.',
"Match your hook to your tone. A hopeful speech might open with an inspiring moment, while a speech about a serious problem might open with a striking fact.",
match('Opening technique and example',[['Vivid story','“The monitor beeped, but no one looked up.”'],['Surprising fact','“One in three patients feels unheard.”'],['Question','“What if good care were measured by listening?”'],['Bold statement','“The old standard of care is no longer enough.”'],['Quote','A brief, relevant line from a respected professional'],['Personal moment','“My grandfather whispered, ‘No one asked me.’”']])]],
['Establishing the topic and point of view',[
'After the hook, connect clearly to the theme: name the standard you are redefining and state your **point of view**. For example: “Today, I want to redefine the standard of care—not as how quickly we treat, but as how well we listen.”',
'This sentence earns points for appropriateness to the theme and clear focus. Make it explicit; do not leave judges guessing.',
'Use the theme’s words naturally rather than repeating them mechanically.',
'Keep this statement short so it is easy to remember.',
'You will echo it in your closing.',
"Make your thesis sentence distinct by delivering it slightly slower and with a pause before it, so judges recognize it as your central message.",
ex('From hook to thesis','Connect a hook to a thesis (example).',['Hook: “My grandfather whispered, ‘No one asked me how I felt.’”','Bridge: “His words revealed a gap in how we define good care.”','Thesis: “Today, I want to redefine the standard of care—from how fast we treat to how well we listen.”'],'A smooth path from attention to point of view.')]],
['Previewing the speech',[
'A brief **preview** tells listeners what to expect: “First, we’ll see why listening matters; second, what gets in the way; and finally, how each of us can raise the standard.” Previews help listeners follow and help judges see your organization.',
'Keep the preview to one or two sentences. In a five-minute speech, a quick roadmap is enough.',
'Use the same words for your points in the preview and in the body so listeners recognize them.',
'Some speakers use a memorable phrase for each point, which also aids your own memory.',
'The preview leads naturally into your first main point.',
"Keep the preview short and parallel, using similar grammatical forms for each point, so it is easy to remember.",
order('Structure of an opening',['Hook that grabs attention','Bridge to the theme','Thesis with point of view','Brief preview of main points','Transition to the first point'])]],
['Timing and delivery of the opening',[
'Keep the opening to about **30 to 45 seconds**. Deliver the first line slowly and clearly, with a pause afterward to let it land. Make eye contact with the judges before you begin speaking.',
'Without a microphone, project from the first word. Stand confidently, take a breath and begin only when you are ready.',
'Memorize your opening lines so you can deliver them with full eye contact rather than reading notecards.',
'A confident start signals preparation and builds credibility.',
'Practice the opening until it feels natural and steady.',
"If nerves make your voice shaky at first, a slower first sentence helps steady it and sounds intentional.",
sort('Strong opening delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Pausing after the first line',0],['Rushing the first sentence',1],['Making eye contact before speaking',0],['Reading the opening from notecards',1],['Projecting from the first word',0]])]],
['Testing your opening',[
'Test the opening with listeners who do not know your speech. After 45 seconds, ask: What is the speech about? What is the speaker’s point of view? Do you want to hear more? If they cannot answer clearly, revise.',
'Try two or three versions and compare reactions.',
'Choose the version that is clearest and most engaging, not necessarily the most dramatic.',
'Revisit the opening after writing the rest of the speech to make sure it still fits.',
'Small wording changes can make a big difference.',
chk('What should listeners be able to say after your opening?',['What the speech is about and your point of view','Your home address','The judges’ names','Nothing'],'Clear topic and stance.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Opening revision',{a:{text:'Your opening is a long dictionary definition of “standard.” (Scenario invented for practice.) How can you improve it?',choices:[['Replace it with a vivid story or question, then state your point of view','b','Correct.',true],['Add a second definition','b','Definitions rarely grab attention.',false]]},b:{text:'Listeners say they were engaged but unsure of your main point. What do you add?',choices:[['A clear thesis sentence connecting to the theme','c','Correct.',true],['More drama','c','Clarity establishes the topic.',false]]},c:{text:'Your opening captures attention and states your message.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The opening (10 points) must grab attention and clearly establish the topic; avoid generic starts like “My speech is about…”','Use a vivid story, surprising fact, thought-provoking question, bold statement or brief quote, then bridge to the theme and state a clear point of view.','Preview two or three main points, keep the opening to 30–45 seconds, memorize and deliver it with eye contact, pauses and projection and test it with listeners.'],terms:[['Hook','An opening device that captures attention.'],['Thesis','A clear statement of your point of view.'],['Preview','A brief roadmap of the speech’s main points.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the opening statement worth?','10','5','15','30','Rubric.'),q('Which opening is strongest?','A vivid moment that leads to your thesis','“Hi, my speech is about standards.”','A long dictionary definition','“Um, okay, so…”','Hook.'),q('What should follow the hook?','A bridge to the theme and a clear point of view','The closing','A random joke','Silence for a minute','Structure.'),q('What does a preview do?','Tells listeners the main points ahead','Ends the speech','Replaces evidence','Lists sources','Roadmap.'),q('About how long should the opening be?','30–45 seconds','2 minutes','5 seconds','4 minutes','Timing.'),q('Why memorize the opening?','To deliver it with eye contact and confidence','Because notes are banned','To make it longer','It is not useful','Delivery.'),q('Which delivery choice is strong?','Pausing after the first line','Rushing','Reading from cards','Looking down','Delivery.'),q('After your opening, listeners should know:','The topic and your point of view','Your address','The judges’ names','Nothing','Clarity.')],frq:{prompt:'Write and explain an opening for your “Redefine the Standard” speech.',rubric:[['Hook',['story','question','fact','bold','attention']],['Theme and thesis',['redefine','standard','point of view','thesis','clear']],['Preview',['preview','first','second','finally','main points']],['Delivery',['30','45 seconds','pause','eye contact','projection','memorize']]],model:'My opening would begin with a vivid moment: “The monitor beeped every few seconds, the nurse typed quickly and the doctor reviewed the chart—but when my grandfather whispered, ‘No one asked me how I feel,’ no one looked up.” I would pause to let that image land, then bridge to the theme: “His words revealed a gap in how we define good care.” Next, I would state my point of view clearly: “Today, I want to redefine the standard of care—from how fast we treat to how well we listen.” Then I would preview my structure in one sentence: “First, we will see why listening is medicine; second, what stands in its way; and finally, how each of us can raise this standard starting today.” This opening grabs attention with a story that creates a mental image without props, clearly establishes the topic and connects directly to “Redefine the Standard,” earning points for the opening, the theme and clear focus. I would keep it to about 40 seconds, memorize it so I can deliver it with full eye contact, take a breath and look at the judges before my first word and project my voice from the start since there are no microphones. I would test it with classmates, asking them to tell me the topic and my point of view after 45 seconds, and revise if they could not.'},sources:[RULES,TM]};
